UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 37, Lesson 1: Being active every day', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"The Australian 24-Hour Movement Guidelines recommend at least 60 minutes of moderate to vigorous physical activity every day for children aged 5 to 13, plus several hours of light activity, and no more than 2 hours of recreational screen time. Moderate activity makes you breathe harder, like brisk walking; vigorous activity makes you puff, like running. Activity can be split into short bursts and done in many ways, including play, sport, walking to school and dancing.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nLayla did 25 minutes of active play at lunch, 15 minutes of walking and 10 minutes of dancing. Add the minutes and say whether she reached 60.\n\nFEEDBACK\n25 + 15 + 10 = 50 minutes. She is 10 minutes short, so she could add a short bike ride or a game after school. Activity can be split into smaller bursts that add up.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking activity only counts if it is organised sport. Walking, playing, dancing and helping with chores can all count.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nKeep a one-day activity log with each activity, the minutes and the effort level (moderate or vigorous). Add the total and compare it with 60 minutes. Plan two new ways to add activity that suit your family and space, including an indoor option for wet days.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"The Australian 24-Hour Movement Guidelines recommend at least 60 minutes of moderate to vigorous physical activity every day for children aged 5 to 13, plus several hours of light activity, and no more than 2 hours of recreational screen time. Moderate activity makes you breathe harder, like brisk walking; vigorous activity makes you puff, like running. Activity can be split into short bursts and done in many ways, including play, sport, walking to school and dancing.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn and discuss","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-MSP-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Keep a one-day activity log with each activity, the minutes and the effort level (moderate or vigorous). Add the total and compare it with 60 minutes. Plan two new ways to add activity that suit your family and space, in Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using daily activity on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking activity only counts if it is organised sport.. Correction: 25 + 15 + 10 = 50 minutes.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking activity only counts if it is organised sport. Walking, playing, dancing and helping with chores can all count. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating daily activity on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with daily activity, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about daily activity with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for daily activity. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for daily activity. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","25 + 15 + 10 = 50 minutes","Thinking activity only counts if it is organised sport"],"question":"Layla did 25 minutes of active play at lunch, 15 minutes of walking and 10 minutes of dancing. Add the minutes and say whether she reached 60. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for daily activity; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["The Australian 24-Hour Movement Guidelines recommend at least 60 minutes of moderate to vigorous physical activity","25 + 15 + 10 = 50 minutes","Careful use of moderate and vigorous and explain why it with clear evidence","Thinking activity only counts if it is organised sport"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with daily activity?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named moderate and vigorous and explain why it, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name moderate and vigorous and explain why it only, without linking it to daily activity","Apply daily activity to new material and name the moderate and vigorous and explain why it used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Thinking activity only counts if it is organised sport"],"question":"Which response best applies daily activity with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses safe, healthy or respectful choices appropriately for this lesson on daily activity. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Thinking activity only counts if it is organised sport","25 + 15 + 10 = 50 minutes"],"question":"Which response best uses safe, healthy or respectful choices for daily activity?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy and say how it helps with daily activity","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Thinking activity only counts if it is organised sport"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about daily activity?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (The Australian 24-Hour Movement Guidelines recommend at least 60) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Example: Aiden''s day. Walk to school: 15 minutes (moderate). Playground tag: 20 minutes (vigorous). PE: 20 minutes","Thinking activity only counts if it is organised sport","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about daily activity?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check safety and fairness, include others, and explain the healthier choice","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster"],"question":"Which action keeps safe, healthy or respectful choices accurate for daily activity?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking activity only counts if it is organised sport","Name moderate and vigorous and explain why it only, with no linked example","Keep a one-day activity log with each activity, the minutes and the effort level (moderate or vigorous)","Retype the worked example with no new example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies daily activity to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about daily activity with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating daily activity on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using daily activity on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for daily activity. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with daily activity, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking activity only counts if it is organised sport.. Correction: 25 + 15 + 10 = 50 minutes.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w37-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise daily activity.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Healthy, Safe and Active Lifestyles","success_criteria":["I can use daily activity with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify moderate and vigorous and explain why it matters.","I can practise 60 minutes independently on a new prompt.","I can notice an activity log needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The Australian 24-Hour Movement Guidelines recommend at least 60 minutes of moderate to vigorous physical activity every day for children aged 5 to 13, plus several hours of light activity, and no more than 2 hours of recreational screen time.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason","The Australian 24-Hour Movement Guidelines recommend at least 60 minutes of moderate to vigorous physical activity"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about daily activity with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Aiden''s day. Walk to school: 15 minutes (moderate). Playground tag: 20 minutes (vigorous). PE: 20 minutes (moderate to vigorous). Bike ride after school: 15 minutes. Total: 70 minutes. He rates effort: walking 3 out of 5, tag 5 out of 5. He keeps recreational screen time to 1 hour.\n\nTRY FIRST\nLayla did 25 minutes of active play at lunch, 15 minutes of walking and 10 minutes of dancing. Add the minutes and say whether she reached 60.\n\nFEEDBACK\n25 + 15 + 10 = 50 minutes. She is 10 minutes short, so she could add a short bike ride or a game after school. Activity can be split into smaller bursts that add up.","explain":"The Australian 24-Hour Movement Guidelines recommend at least 60 minutes of moderate to vigorous physical activity every day for children aged 5 to 13, plus several hours of light activity, and no more than 2 hours of recreational screen time. Moderate activity makes you breathe harder, like brisk walking; vigorous activity makes you puff, like running. Activity can be split into short bursts and done in many ways, including play, sport, walking to school and dancing.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking activity only counts if it is organised sport. Walking, playing, dancing and helping with chores can all count. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Moderate and vigorous activity.","caption":"Both count.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Moderate</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">brisk walk, breathe harder</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Vigorous</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">running, puffing</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Aiden''s day. Walk to school: 15 minutes (moderate). Playground tag: 20 minutes (vigorous). PE: 20 minutes (mode Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Aiden''s day. Walk to school: 15 minutes (moderate). Playground tag: 20 minutes (vigorous). PE: 20 minutes (mode Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about daily activity with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Aiden''s day. Walk to school: 15 minutes (moderate). Playground tag: 20 minutes (vigorous). PE: 20 minutes (moderate to vigorous). Bike ride after school: 15 minutes. Total: 70 minutes. He rates effort: walking 3 out of 5, tag 5 out of 5. He keeps recreational screen time to 1 hour. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Adding minutes of activity.","caption":"Add the bursts up.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Total</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">25 + 15 + 10 = 50</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Goal</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">60 minutes a day</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison.","options":["Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about daily activity with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking activity only counts if it is organised sport. Walking, playing, dancing and helping with chores can all count. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Aiden''s day. Walk to school: 15 minutes (moderate). Playground tag: 20 minutes (vigorous). PE: 20 minutes (moderate to vigorous). Bike ride after school: 15 minutes. Total: 70 minutes. He rates effort: walking 3 out of 5, tag 5 out of 5. He keeps recreational screen time to 1 hour.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\n25 + 15 + 10 = 50 minutes. She is 10 minutes short, so she could add a short bike ride or a game after school. Activity can be split into smaller bursts that add up.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking activity only counts if it is organised sport. Walking, playing, dancing and helping with chores can all count. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example.","options":["Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about daily activity with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThe Australian 24-Hour Movement Guidelines recommend at least 60 minutes of moderate to vigorous physical activity every day for children aged 5 to 13, plus several hours of light activity, and no more than 2 hours of recreational screen time. Moderate activity makes you breathe harder, like brisk walking; vigorous activity makes you puff, like running. Activity can be split into short bursts and done in many ways, including play, sport, walking to school and dancing. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 37, Lesson 1: Being active every day","unit_number":8,"unit_title":"Healthy habits","week":37,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Aiden''s day. Walk to school: 15 minutes (moderate). Playground tag: 20 minutes (vigorous). PE: 20 minutes (moderate to vigorous). Bike ride after school: 15 minutes. Total: 70 minutes. He rates effort: walking 3 out of 5, tag 5 out of 5. He keeps recreational screen time to 1 hour.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Moderate and vigorous activity.","caption":"Both count.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Moderate</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">brisk walk, breathe harder</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Vigorous</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">running, puffing</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Adding minutes of activity.","caption":"Add the bursts up.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=